  abstract={The rise of Vaporfly tech (VFT) by Nike at year 2017 has supposed a disruption in long distance races. By using this kind of shoes an unexpected number of records in all standardized road distances have been broken. Scientific literature indicates that VFT improves performance, at least, in high trained athletes. This reason explains the deep ethical discussion about its use in competitions and the set of norms approved at 2020 by World Athletics regulating the use of this tech. This paper analyses how ethical factors of multiple dimension ethical scale (MES) by Reidenbach and Robin (1990) revised in Shawver and Sennetti (2009) influence the acceptance of VFT shoes. That acceptance is measured with the scale proposed by Venkatesh and Davis for intention to use a tech. Relations between variables has been fitted with a structural equation model that has been adjusted with partial least squares. So, the influence of every explanatory variable on acceptance is measured with so-called coefficient of the path (cp) whose value lies within [-1, 1] being its interpretation, the same as a correlation coefficient. We have found that relativism (cp=0.388, p&lt;0.01) and egoism (cp=0.241, p&lt;0.01) are clearly relevant ethical variables to explain acceptance of VFT shoes. Likewise, also moral equity (cp=0.12, p=0.262) and utilitarianism (cp=0.098, p=0.13) present a positive relation with attitude towards that tech. However, these relations are not significant at standard statistical levels. Likewise, we have also found that contractualism present a negative non-significant relation with intention to use Vaporfly tech (VFT).},
